  list  i recommend choosing an option that is  unsweetened  because some non-dairy milks have 10 or  more grams of added sugar  per cup and i would wait rather people  be able to control the amount of sugar  in their drinks  by adding sweetener at their discretion  you'll also want to find one that's  fortified with calcium  b12 and vitamin d which makes it more  comparable to  dairy milk and also ideally one with  higher amounts of protein  so a lot of nut based coconut rice or  oat milk for example  will be pretty low in protein maybe a  gram per serving  but something like soy milk or pea milk  will have  higher amounts now ingredient wise a lot  of non-dairy milks will have additives  and thickeners like guar gum or  carrageenan which really helps to  prevent the solids and the water from  separating  i know that these are super  controversial so i'm going to keep it  really short  but if you do want a whole video on  additives and thickeners  let me know and i will add it to my list  so  guar gum is a binding agent that's high  in soluble fiber  so it actually may help promote satiety  and regulate blood sugar the main thing  to flag however  is that large amounts can obstruct the  digestive system  but since the fda heavily regulates how  much gore gum can be added to a food  the worst you'll likely see is a bit of  gas diarrhea  bloating and cramps full blockages  aren't really a concern carrageenan on  the other hand comes from  red seaweed and some researchers have  presented animal research on it having  harmful effects on the gi tract  but then equally respected researchers  have refuted this science  and suggests that there's no reason to  ban it as a food additive  so i would say that because the research  isn't very clear  if you have a sensitive gut or ibs you  may want to look for a non-dairy milk  without these emulsifiers  just to be safe as for environmental  concerns  compared to cow's milk soy and oat milk  use  far less water land and produce less  carbon emissions  and while rice and almond are often  called out for being water hogs  they're still considered more  environmentally friendly than  cow's milk so to recap if you're going  dairy free  i recommend looking for a non-dairy milk  that is unsweetened  fortified with d c and ideally b12  one without emulsifiers if your gut is  sensitive  one that tastes good to you and one that  is environmentally friendly  especially if you drink a lot of it i  honestly switch things up and kind of go  through phases that are largely based on  flavor  right now as strange as it seems i've  been mixing  unsweetened cashew milk with a little  bit of chocolate oat milk in my lattes  but other days i use straight up  unsweetened almond milk and  that's kind of what i do so choose one  that works best for you  all right next dear abby how much  protein do  you actually need oh this  is a controversial topic and also a  really hard one to answer straight up  because obviously everybody's needs are  just so  variable and different if you look at  the recommended daily allowance or rda  for protein we're looking at 0.8  grams per kilogram so if i were to weigh  150 pounds  that would mean just 55 grams of protein  that's literally a chicken breast but  that's also pretty much the bare minimum  and most recommendations are much higher  to account for greater levels of  physical activity  as well as optimal body composition and  satiety  when i spoke to chelsea cross she told  me that a lot of people are scared to  get too much protein  but the research repeatedly suggests  that higher intakes  do not adversely affect kidney function  or bone health  in normal healthy adults so as far as  both of us are concerned  0.8 grams per kilogram is not adequate  to sustain muscle mass  for anyone who exercises or is trying to  manage their weight  but let's talk about some of the various  numbers and recommendations  that you might see floating around so in  2009  the academy of dietetics dietitians of  canada and american college of sports  medicine  all suggested that individuals who do 10  or more hours of vigorous endurance  training weekly  would need 1.2 to 1.4 grams per kilogram  and those who are training for strength  would need 1.4 to 1.8 grams per kilogram  another authority the national academy  of sports medicine  said that weight training athletes would  need 1.5 to  2 grams per kilogram per day and finally  a 2018 paper from the journal of  international society  of sports nutrition recommended 1.6  grams per kilogram  for muscle building so clearly lots of  different numbers  and it really comes down to your unique  needs and your unique  goals but now let's look at some of the  specific research for specific  populations well for muscle building  mass  in postmenopausal women one study  suggested that muscle strength improved  in women who are consuming a minimum of  1.2 grams per kilogram per day  in middle-aged adults trying to lose  weight research found that those who  consumed at least  1.34 grams per kilogram lost  more weight than those who ate the same  amount of calories  but lower amounts of protein in athletes  who are attempting  fat loss a protein intake of 1.8 to 2  grams per  kilogram has been shown to promote  weight loss while minimizing muscle loss  and research on fit young people has  found that ranges between  1.6 to 1.8 grams per kilogram  are ideal for body composition but that  anything beyond that  does not confer any additional benefits  so  what number is it abby well i don't  believe there is  one firm number for everyone i did ask  my colleague chelsea cross what she  recommends based on her own clinical  experience  working with the population for whom  protein is very important  she said she sticks to 0.8 to 1 gram per  pound of body weight  or 1.8 to 2.2 grams per kilogram of body  weight  with 1.6 grams per kilogram being the  minimum  for normal healthy adults her rationale  for aiming a bit higher is that  while excess protein may not promote  muscle protein synthesis  higher protein diets have been shown to  be more effective  and favorable for weight management  because they're just more satiating  and support metabolic muscle mass hey  guys just a little update from  postpartum life  as i was re-watching this in editing i  did have a thought that  i really should have included my own  professional opinion  even though sports nutrition really  isn't my area of expertise  but i want to clarify that i think most  average joes  probably don't need that much protein i  mean it's important for me to reiterate  that chelsea does  work largely with a population  interested in fitness and body  composition  which is really what informs her  recommendations at large  but if this isn't a priority for you  then  getting up to these numbers is likely  unnecessary  1.6 grams per kilogram is twice the rda  minimum recommendation so obviously  there is a wide range of appropriate  protein intakes  depending on your unique goals i also  want to add that unless you're an  athlete or  do have specific nutrition goals you  don't ever need to be counting grams of  protein  so as a general rule of thumb i suggest  aiming to fill about a quarter of your  plate with protein  about the size of the palm of your hand  and then aiming for at least  some protein at most snacks to help  boost up the satiety factor  bottom line this is not a  one-size-fits-all  model some people will just feel better  consuming  more or less protein depending on their  activity  and their metabolism so if you really  want to know the best amount of protein  for you  i really suggest working with a  dietitian not relying on  online calculators or even youtube  videos like this one to determine  your unique needs okay back to pregnant  abby  dear abby is the whoosh effect  during weight loss backed up by any  scientific literature  okay so if you haven't heard of the  woosh effect  it's essentially the idea that if you're  following a diet  one day you wake up and whoosh the  weight is gone  it's kind of like a massive breakthrough  in a weight loss plateau  so this is often talked about with  respect to the keto diet  but some people also find that it occurs  with other weight loss  diets as well now the idea behind this  is that when you start a diet your fat  cells  start to lose fat and that fat is  apparently replaced with water  and then one day whoosh that water's  flushed out into the toilet  and the fat cells allegedly collapse and  you drop  a ton of weight that's a theory anyways  the truth is  i searched and i searched and i searched  and i asked every single dietitian in  all of my practice groups  and there is no evidence that fat is  replaced with water when you lose weight  what we do know is that when you put  yourself in a caloric deficit for long  enough  the triglycerides in your cells are  broken down into carbon dioxide  and water you exhale the carbon dioxide  and you would pee breathe or sweat out  the water  specifically when it comes to keto we  also do see some  rapid weight loss at the start of a  program because you end up draining your  glycogen stores  aka your carb stores and for every one  gram of carbs  you hold on to about three to four grams  of water  so when you drain those carb stores you  reduce water retention  and that weight loss results in a lot of  the early  weight loss on keto now i can appreciate  the anecdotal evidence of the woosh  effect  is really powerful stuff and that even  coaches and fitness professionals  swear that they do see this whoosh-like  effect on the scale  after a weight loss stall but there's no  scientific way to explain this  as it relates to fat cells filling up  with water  and therefore it's likely that the  rationale for breakthroughs in weight  loss plateaus  are highly individual some alternative  hypotheses  is that it may be related to depleted  carb stores  hormonal shifts or your body just  adjusting to a lower caloric level  after it was fighting to maintain its  set point for so long  but ultimately we really don't have any  research on the woosh effect  or any of these other alternative  hypotheses to fully explain  why some people may experience this  phenomenon  all right next dear abby what is the  fine line between intermittent fasting  and disordered eating very good  question so good i want to hear from my  colleagues so  i posed this to one of my dietitian  practice groups and we all had such  an interesting discussion a lot of rds  said that there was no line  and that any restrictive diet was  disordered in nature  i tend to think it really depends on the  person  and how the diet impacts their life and  this could be applied not just to  intermittent fasting but really  to any intentional weight loss diet here  are some things to consider when  determining if a diet or way of eating  is unhealthy for you and your  relationship with food  ask yourself these questions is this  diet allowing you to get  everything you need both nutritionally  and emotionally  does this diet interfere with your  enjoyment of life or daily activities  do you feel stress or anxiety if you  break the rules  or you don't see the progress that  you're looking for does breaking your  diet throw you into even more  restrictive and panicked thinking  or can you just pick it up back where  you left off whenever you see fit  does this diet feel like it's become  part of your identity  and your worth and your character and  all of those things are bound up in your  ability to stick to the rules  is this diet feeling like punishment  because you hate your body or because  you love your body and just feel  better making these changes i genuinely  believe that there are a lot of  non-punishing reasons to try a keto diet  or intermittent fasting  or low fat diet etc if it benefits one's  physical  and emotional health it really comes  down to intentions  and how this way of eating makes you  feel about your body  food and your self-worth all right next  dear abby does vitamin c really  strengthen the immune system  okay so since i was a little girl every  time my dad would feel a cold coming on  he would literally down a bottle of  vitamin c tablets  vitamin c is a powerful antioxidant that  helps immune cells function  and it also helps to support physical  barriers like epithelial cells in your  skin  which protect you from pathogens and  while over a hundred animal studies  have found that vitamin c  supplementation can help prevent  infections by microorganisms does this  apply to humans well in a 2013 cochrane  review  it was found that people who already had  symptoms of a cold  did not get any benefit from vitamin c  though some studies suggest that  megadoses may help to reduce the  duration  we also need more research on that  the review also found that adults who  take vitamin c  regularly can expect eight percent  shorter colds  and maybe less severe symptoms while  athletes who take vitamin c regularly  are fifty percent less likely to catch a  cold than athletes who don't  another 2018 meta-analysis with nine  trials  found that vitamin c could shorten colds  and reduce symptoms  but that this meta-analysis was a third  of the size of the cochlear view  so it's not necessarily considered as  strong of evidence  bottom line supplementing regularly with  vitamin c  may help shorten the duration of a cold  and maybe even help prevent them in  athletes  but if you start taking it when you're  already sick  you're probably out of luck so  officially i don't like to say that  vitamin c  or really any one supplement boosts your  immune system  but it may help to support your immune  system  through its powerful antioxidant  properties okay and  last question for today's video dear  abby  do collagen supplements actually do  anything  very good question because man i am just  waiting for that postpartum hair loss to  kick in  and i am not looking forward to it  but collagen is one of the most abundant  proteins in the body  and makes up 80 of our skin  and not surprisingly as we age our  bodies naturally start producing  less collagen and that's why my friends  we have botox  but collagen supplements are found as  hydrolyzed collagen aka collagen  peptides which are  broken down to make it easier for the  body to absorb gelatin  which is partially broken down into  amino acids and as  raw or undenatured where the protein is  left  intact let's talk about some of the  alleged claims  starting with skin health now research  suggests that taking collagen  supplements for  several months can help to improve  wrinkles  and roughness in skin and reduce some of  the common signs of aging  one study in middle aged women found  that taking 2.5 grams  of hydrolyzed collagen daily for 8 weeks  reduced wrinkle volume  by 20 percent now another study found  that taking 2.5 grams of another brand  for 12 weeks reduced wrinkle depth by 27  and increased skin hydration by 28  sounds great yes but keep in mind that  these studies were funded by the  companies themselves  so it would be so great to see some more  third party testing  and some more randomized control trials  next let's talk about  hair so the research in this area is  limited  but one industry-funded double-blind  randomized control trial found that  15 healthy women with self-perceived  thinning hair  who are given a marine based collagen  supplement experienced subjective  improvements in hair volume sculpt  coverage  and thickness after 90 days as for nails  i wasn't able to find more than just one  study that used a brand of collagen  peptides on  25 participants but it found that it  promoted an increase in 12  nail growth rate and a decrease in 42  percent  in broken nails so while these study  results may seem really promising  there are a few really important red  flags to note  one the sample sizes are typically  really small  two they're funded by the product  manufacturers and  three these products often have various  ingredients  so it is hard to know exactly if it's  the collagen specifically  playing a beneficial role next let's  talk about joint health  so collagen type 2 is found in cartilage  which  is that cushioning that we have between  our joints we do have  industry funded research to suggest that  certain brands  can help to reduce joint pain stiffness  and disability in people with  osteoarthritis  by up to 38 but a systematic review of  the literature suggested  that the impact that it may have on  joint pain is small  at best so it's hard to know how  meaningful these results are to patients  in this grand scheme of everything what  about bones  well bones are made largely up of type 1  collagen  so it makes sense that consuming more  collagen may help to protect against  osteoporosis while most studies have  been conducted on animals  one human study on post-menopausal women  found that taking a brand of hydrolyzed  collagen  for a year increased bone density by  three to seven percent  bottom line collagen supplements may  help with a  wide range of ailments and it  theoretically  does make sense that they should but i  don't think that we have  a lot of really strong non-industry  funded research  yet to conclusively say that they make a  significant impact  also even if we did keep in mind that  the supplement world  isn't well regulated so i always suggest  looking  up your supplement brand on a  third-party testing website  like labdoor.com to see how your  supplement brand ranks  for purity and contaminants and that is  all for me today folks  thank you again to trade for sponsoring  this video if you liked it  be sure to give it the thumbs up leave  me a comment below with any of your  burning nutrition related questions  don't forget to subscribe to the channel  and i will see you next time on abby's  kitchen  bye  you
 

